  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2025 article is already entering scholarly conversation: OpenAlex records 1 citation, a downstream reuse in a preprint, and one teaching-adjacent mention in a YouTube video. The available evidence suggests early uptake in philosophy of mind and cognitive science, with attention concentrated in a small but relevant set of follow-on work.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Blaise Ag\u00fcera y Arcas\u2019s <em>What Is Intelligence?</em> has begun to register as a conceptual reference point for work on intelligence, prediction, and cognition. The article is cited once in the current record, and that citation comes from a 2025 preprint, indicating an initial scholarly reception rather than a broad citation footprint. The citing work sits in themes such as philosophy and theoretical science, embodied and extended cognition, and cognitive science, which aligns with the article\u2019s argument that intelligence can be understood through predictive processes.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The reuse record adds another sign of early influence. One downstream preprint is documented as reusing the work, suggesting that its framing is being taken up in adjacent theoretical discussion. This kind of reuse is modest in scale, but it is meaningful for a recent article with a conceptually ambitious thesis: the record shows the paper being used as a building block in subsequent argumentation rather than remaining a standalone commentary.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also a small teaching-adoption signal. A YouTube video on the Antikythera channel discusses the article, which indicates that the piece has already moved into broader explanatory or pedagogical circulation. The record does not support treating this as formal curriculum adoption, but it does show that the article is being used to introduce or frame ideas about intelligence for a wider audience.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the impact profile is still early and selective. The evidence points to a work that is beginning to influence theoretical conversations at the intersection of psychology and computer science, with one citation, one documented reuse, and one recorded discussion in a video format. That combination is consistent with a recently published article whose main impact is presently conceptual: it is being read, discussed, and used, even though the citation base remains small.</p>",
